      "evidence": {
        "state": "measured",
        "costBytesByTier": {
          "q4": 167639366,
          "q8": 167639366
        },
        "source": "config/manifests/builtin.models.jsonc — chroma1_base tier subdirs bundle a dense VAE; only the transformer is quantized per tier; isolated artifact: SceneWorks/chroma1-base-mlx@e7330dda29d00ffdeeb719b28e92ee74cff0884c:q8/vae/diffusion_pytorch_model.safetensors",
        "isolation": "Summed the exact safetensors-header tensor byte spans for q8/vae/diffusion_pytorch_model.safetensors at immutable revision e7330dda29d00ffdeeb719b28e92ee74cff0884c; all 244 tensors are BF16 and mlx-gen loads the AutoencoderKL at that native width, so floating tensors are counted at 2 bytes, not 4. This is isolated weight residency, excluding activations and allocator overhead.",
        "triageDecision": "eliminate",
        "eliminationStory": "sc-16462",
        "reviewedCause": "packing-exception",
        "triageNote": "Eliminate under sc-16462: the mlx lane can pack this component to the selected tier exactly as mlx-gen-flux's `prequantize_turnkey` already does for the SAME T5-XXL and AutoencoderKL (flux1_* carry no textEncoder row at all). This supersedes the sc-15799 note, which read the since-deleted provider comment \"a measurably-0% memory-only win\" as a zero-QUALITY-cost claim; the codebase idiom (mlx-gen-sana uses the same phrase for the DC-AE decoder's Linear-attention) means a zero-MEMORY win. The measurement below accounts for the currently shipping bf16 artifact until that repack lands."
      },
      "backends": [
        "mlx"
      ]
    },
    {
      "model": "chroma1_base",
      "component": "vae",
      "residentTier": "f32",
      "appliesToTiers": [
        "q4",
        "q8"
      ],
      "cause": "packing-exception",
      "reason": "Chroma packs only the transformer, and candle-gen-chroma builds the FLUX.1 AutoencoderKL through its f32 VarBuilder, so the dense decoder is resident at f32 rather than its native bf16.",
      "evidence": {
        "state": "measured",
        "costBytesByTier": {
          "q4": 335278732,
          "q8": 335278732
        },
        "source": "crates/media/candle-gen/candle-gen-chroma/src/pipeline.rs — `Vae::new(self.f32_vb(&self.root.join(\"vae\"))?)`; isolated artifact: SceneWorks/chroma1-base-mlx@e7330dda29d00ffdeeb719b28e92ee74cff0884c:q8/vae/diffusion_pytorch_model.safetensors",
        "isolation": "Summed the exact safetensors-header tensor byte spans for q8/vae/diffusion_pytorch_model.safetensors at immutable revision e7330dda29d00ffdeeb719b28e92ee74cff0884c; all 244 tensors are BF16 on disk, but the candle lane builds the AutoencoderKL through `f32_vb`, so floating tensors are counted at 4 bytes for THIS lane while the mlx sibling counts 2. The element counts are the same artifact's and are shared with that row. This is isolated weight residency, excluding activations and allocator overhead.",
        "triageDecision": "measure",
        "reviewedCause": "packing-exception",
        "triageNote": "Measure, not eliminate: the extra width over the bf16 artifact is a candle load-dtype decision (`f32_vb`), not an unpacked artifact, so removing it is a CUDA-lane compute change (tracked as sc-17439) rather than the repack sc-16462 owns for the mlx sibling.",
        "note": "RESTORED by sc-17395. sc-16462 correctly halved the mlx figure (the T5/VAE are bf16 on disk and mlx-gen loads them at that native width, not f32) but then deleted the f32 fact entirely, on the premise that \"the catalog hosts no `candle` block for any chroma entry, so that residency is unreachable\". A manifest per-backend block is optional tuning config, not a lane declaration: chroma1_* is `candle_routed: true` in crates/sceneworks-core/src/jobs_store/routing/catalog.rs and `candle_gen_chroma::register_providers` is wired at candle-gen-catalog/src/lib.rs, so the candle lane ships. The f32 residency is therefore REACHABLE and was undeclared between sc-16462 and sc-17395 — the exact defect the invariant forbids. Split per lane on the SenseNova precedent rather than dropped: declaring bf16 for both would under-declare candle, f32 for both would over-declare mlx (which is what sc-16462 found). See the mlx sibling row."
      },
      "backends": [
        "candle"
      ]
    },
